{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>A day for a year, a day for a year.</b> It should have said 'a year for a day, a year for a day' — that is, I am punishing you and decreeing upon you that you shall be tossed about in the wilderness like a shepherd's tent that is moved from place to place, one year for each and every day that you spied out the land; and according to the logic of the matter it should have put the punishment first. But it comes to inform us that from within His punishment and His rebuke we learn mercy. And when He said 'a day for a year, a day for a year,' He alluded to what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: now is not the land of Israel four hundred parasang by four hundred parasang? And how were they able to traverse it in forty days? Rather, it was revealed and known before the One who spoke and the world came into being that He was destined to decree upon them a year for a day, and so He shortened the way for them. Therefore He said 'a day for a year, a day for a year' — like a father striking his son, not the blow of a cruel one but with the compassion of a father for his children; its meaning is: I have shortened the way for you, so that My oath might be diminished."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:32.135279947Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}